PNT.registerModule({"id": "m07", "number": 7, "title": "Resilient PNT Engineering", "subtitle": "", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Distinguish redundancy, diversity, dissimilarity, independence, failover, holdover and recovery.", "Translate operational PNT needs into measurable resilience requirements.", "Explain resistance, detection, response and recovery as complementary resilience functions.", "Use recovery point and recovery time objectives to express mission-driven PNT recovery needs.", "Explain the functions of a layered resilient-PNT reference architecture.", "Understand source monitoring, fault detection, exclusion and isolation at a systems-engineering level.", "Explain why sensor fusion must be integrity-aware rather than simply combine every available source.", "Design normal, degraded, restricted, unavailable and recovery modes.", "Explain failover criteria, transition behaviour and the role of uncertainty during source loss.", "Explain why recovery requires revalidation rather than immediate reuse of a returning source.", "Identify hidden common-mode dependencies and single points of failure in apparently redundant architectures.", "Apply the architecture to navigation and timing failure scenarios and define appropriate evidence for assurance."], "lessons": [{"id": "from-pnt-resilience-theory-to-engineering", "title": "From PNT Resilience Theory to Engineering", "html": "<p>Module 6 examined how PNT can be denied, degraded or deceived. Module 7 asks the engineering question that follows: how should a system be designed so that a single disruption does not immediately become mission failure?</p>\n<p>Resilient PNT is not achieved by adding a second receiver and calling it a backup. A robust design begins with operational requirements, uses appropriately independent sources, monitors whether each source remains trustworthy, manages uncertainty, transitions deliberately between modes and defines how confidence is restored after a failure.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Working definition</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Resilient PNT is the engineered ability to protect critical PNT capability from harm when adverse events occur. A useful functional model separates passive resistance from the active functions of detection, response and recovery. Resilience is a matter of degree against defined threats and mission requirements, not an absolute property.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m07-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Distinguish redundancy, diversity, dissimilarity, independence, failover, holdover and recovery.</p></li>\n<li><p>Translate operational PNT needs into measurable resilience requirements.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain resistance, detection, response and recovery as complementary resilience functions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use recovery point and recovery time objectives to express mission-driven PNT recovery needs.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain the functions of a layered resilient-PNT reference architecture.</p></li>\n<li><p>Understand source monitoring, fault detection, exclusion and isolation at a systems-engineering level.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why sensor fusion must be integrity-aware rather than simply combine every available source.</p></li>\n<li><p>Design normal, degraded, restricted, unavailable and recovery modes.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain failover criteria, transition behaviour and the role of uncertainty during source loss.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why recovery requires revalidation rather than immediate reuse of a returning source.</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify hidden common-mode dependencies and single points of failure in apparently redundant architectures.</p></li>\n<li><p>Apply the architecture to navigation and timing failure scenarios and define appropriate evidence for assurance.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "start-with-the-mission-not-the-technology", "title": "Start with the Mission, Not the Technology", "html": "<p>A resilient architecture cannot be judged without knowing what capability must survive and for how long. “Backup GPS” is a technology statement. “Maintain horizontal position within 20 m for 15 minutes after loss of trusted GNSS, with a warning within 5 seconds if the bound cannot be maintained” is an engineering requirement.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Requirement dimension</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Questions to define</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT function</td>\n<td>Position, velocity, attitude, navigation, frequency, absolute time, or a combination?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Required performance</td>\n<td>Accuracy, integrity, availability, continuity, update rate and confidence/bounds?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Outage / threat duration</td>\n<td>Seconds, minutes, hours or days? Continuous or intermittent?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Detection</td>\n<td>How quickly must denial, degradation or deception be recognised?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Degraded capability</td>\n<td>What reduced performance is still useful and safe?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>What evidence is required before returning to normal mode?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Environment</td>\n<td>Urban, maritime, airborne, indoor, underground, contested RF, remote, fixed infrastructure?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Consequence</td>\n<td>What happens if PNT is unavailable versus plausible but wrong?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineering principle</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Resilience is requirement-relative. An architecture can be resilient for one mission and inadequate for another because the required performance, outage duration and consequences differ.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "redundancy-diversity-dissimilarity-and-independence", "title": "Redundancy, Diversity, Dissimilarity and Independence", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Concept</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Meaning</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Redundancy</td>\n<td>More than one component/source can perform a required function.</td>\n<td>Two GNSS receivers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Diversity</td>\n<td>Alternatives differ in source, technology, implementation or supplier.</td>\n<td>GNSS plus terrestrial radio PNT.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Dissimilarity</td>\n<td>Alternatives rely on materially different physical principles or failure mechanisms.</td>\n<td>GNSS radio ranging plus inertial dead reckoning plus visual/terrain matching.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Independence</td>\n<td>A failure affecting one source is sufficiently unlikely to invalidate the alternative through shared dependencies.</td>\n<td>Independent timing path with separate reference, transport, power and local holdover.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Failover</td>\n<td>Controlled transition to an alternate capability after failure/suspicion.</td>\n<td>Reject GNSS and continue on INS/odometry under a defined degraded mode.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Holdover / bridging</td>\n<td>Continue a capability temporarily after losing its external reference.</td>\n<td>Local clock holds UTC estimate; INS propagates navigation state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Controlled restoration of normal operation after the failed/suspect source returns.</td>\n<td>Revalidate returning GNSS before allowing it to influence the fused solution.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Redundancy is necessary but not sufficient</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Two components that share the same antenna, power, software, upstream clock or RF vulnerability can fail together. Resilience improves when redundancy is combined with meaningful diversity, dissimilarity and dependency analysis.</div></aside>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Redundancy, Diversity, Dissimilarity and Independence\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m07-image1.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "defence-in-depth-for-pnt", "title": "Defence in Depth for PNT", "html": "<p>A robust architecture uses several layers rather than assuming any one source or protection mechanism is infallible.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Layer</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Purpose</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source diversity</td>\n<td>Provide alternative observations when a source is lost or untrusted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Protection</td>\n<td>Reduce exposure through installation, configuration, cyber controls, appropriate antennas/equipment and service design.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Monitoring</td>\n<td>Measure source quality, freshness, uncertainty and health continuously.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Detection and isolation</td>\n<td>Identify inconsistent or suspect sources and prevent them contaminating the solution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Fusion / selection</td>\n<td>Use suitable measurements to maintain the best trustworthy state estimate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrity / uncertainty</td>\n<td>Determine whether the current solution meets the application requirement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Mode management</td>\n<td>Control normal, degraded, restricted, failover and recovery behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Application adaptation</td>\n<td>Limit or inhibit functions when PNT confidence is insufficient.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery and evidence</td>\n<td>Re-establish trust and retain evidence for assurance/incident learning.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"m07-resistance-detection-response-and-recovery\">4.1 Resistance, Detection, Response and Recovery</h2>\n<p>The resilience material supplied for this course provides a useful engineering decomposition. Passive resistance reduces the harm a threat can cause, while active functions detect the event or its effects, respond to preserve critical capability, and recover trusted operation afterwards. This avoids treating resilience as a single feature.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Resilience function</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Engineering purpose</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Illustrative examples</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Resistance</td>\n<td>Reduce susceptibility or limit the effect before a mode transition is required.</td>\n<td>Robust installation, appropriate antenna/RF protection, hardened configuration, local holdover capability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Detection</td>\n<td>Recognise loss of availability, performance or trust.</td>\n<td>Integrity monitoring, residuals, cross-source comparison, source-health alarms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Response</td>\n<td>Contain the problem and preserve useful mission capability.</td>\n<td>Exclude suspect data, fail over, enter degraded/restricted mode, notify consumers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Restore and revalidate trusted capability after the disturbance.</td>\n<td>Reacquire, compare with independent truth, acceptance checks, controlled reintegration.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Resilience is threat-relative</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A design may detect one class of disturbance very well yet have weak response or recovery for another. State the threat conditions, critical capability and required behaviour rather than describing the whole system simply as resilient.</div></aside>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Defence in Depth for PNT\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m07-image2.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "source-monitoring-before-fusion", "title": "Source Monitoring Before Fusion", "html": "<p>A resilient system should characterise each source before allowing it to influence the solution. “Available” is not the same as “healthy”. A receiver may output coordinates while its measurements are stale, inconsistent or outside the conditions for which the required performance was demonstrated.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Availability and freshness: is new data arriving at the required rate?</p></li>\n<li><p>Quality: are signal/data quality indicators within expected bounds?</p></li>\n<li><p>Uncertainty: what error or confidence estimate accompanies the measurement?</p></li>\n<li><p>Plausibility: is the value physically possible given the platform state?</p></li>\n<li><p>Consistency: does it agree with independent sources within expected uncertainty?</p></li>\n<li><p>Integrity/authentication: are source-specific warnings or validation features satisfied?</p></li>\n<li><p>Configuration and calibration: is the source correctly aligned, referenced and configured?</p></li>\n<li><p>Age and mode: is the source operating in the state assumed by the performance claim?</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Garbage in, confidently fused out</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A sophisticated fusion algorithm cannot create trustworthy PNT if bad measurements are accepted without appropriate monitoring and fault handling.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "fault-detection-exclusion-and-isolation", "title": "Fault Detection, Exclusion and Isolation", "html": "<p>Fault Detection and Exclusion (FDE), and the broader idea of Fault Detection and Isolation (FDI), describe the functions used to recognise inconsistent information and prevent a faulty source from continuing to corrupt the solution.</p>\n<p>At a systems level, detection can use measurement residuals, cross-source comparisons, physical constraints, integrity indicators, timing checks and source-specific health information. Once a source is suspect, the architecture may reject individual measurements, quarantine the entire source or transition operating mode.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Fault Detection, Exclusion and Isolation\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m07-image3.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Detection is not attribution</p><div class=\"callout-body\">The architecture may need to reject GNSS because it is inconsistent without first proving whether the cause was spoofing, receiver fault, multipath or bad correction data. Safe behaviour should not depend on immediate root-cause attribution.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "worked-reference-architecture", "title": "Worked Reference Architecture", "html": "<p>The diagram below is a teaching reference pattern, not a mandated product architecture. It shows the functions a robust resilient-PNT design should consider and, crucially, where trust decisions are made.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>GNSS<br/>\n</strong>Absolute PVT / time</th>\n<th><strong>INS / IMU<br/>\n</strong>Continuous relative state</th>\n<th><strong>Terrestrial PNT<br/>\n</strong>Dissimilar external reference</th>\n<th><strong>Odometry / vision<br/>\n</strong>Platform/environment constraint</th>\n<th><strong>Clock / network time<br/>\n</strong>Timing + holdover</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>↓ ↓ ↓ ↓ ↓</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">SOURCE-SPECIFIC MONITORING &amp; CHARACTERISATION</p><div class=\"callout-body\">\nSignal/data quality • freshness • uncertainty • calibration • source health</div></aside>\n<p>↓</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">FAULT DETECTION, EXCLUSION &amp; ISOLATION (FDE/FDI)</p><div class=\"callout-body\">\nCross-source consistency • plausibility • residuals • threat/integrity indicators • reject/quarantine suspect data</div></aside>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>FUSION / SOURCE SELECTION</strong><br/>\nUse only sources currently judged suitable</th>\n<th><strong>UNCERTAINTY &amp; INTEGRITY ASSESSMENT</strong><br/>\nEstimate confidence and whether requirements are met</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">ASSURED PNT SERVICE TO APPLICATION</p><div class=\"callout-body\">\nPVT/time + uncertainty + integrity state + source/mode status + age + alerts</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">SUPERVISORY PNT MANAGER / ORCHESTRATOR</p><div class=\"callout-body\">\nControls source enablement, health state, mode transitions, failover, holdover, degraded operation, alerts and recovery/revalidation criteria. It does not simply pick the source with the smallest advertised accuracy.</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Architectural takeaway</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Resilience comes from the behaviour of the whole chain. Multiple sources are useful only if the architecture can monitor them, identify bad information, transition safely, expose uncertainty to the application and recover trust under defined criteria.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "the-pnt-manager-and-state-machine", "title": "The PNT Manager and State Machine", "html": "<p>A resilient system benefits from explicit supervisory logic that knows which mode the PNT service is in and what transitions are permitted. This can be implemented in many ways, but the behaviour should be deliberate, testable and visible to consuming applications.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Mode</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Illustrative entry condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Expected behaviour</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>NORMAL</td>\n<td>Primary and complementary sources healthy; integrity requirement met.</td>\n<td>Full capability. Fusion uses approved sources; normal uncertainty/integrity reporting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>DEGRADED</td>\n<td>Primary source lost or one source quarantined, but requirement still met.</td>\n<td>Continue using alternatives; increase uncertainty; alert application/operator.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>RESTRICTED</td>\n<td>Only reduced performance can be assured.</td>\n<td>Limit autonomy, speed, precision, geographic scope or functions to match remaining PNT capability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>UNAVAILABLE / SAFE</td>\n<td>Integrity or minimum performance cannot be maintained.</td>\n<td>Inhibit PNT-dependent function or transition to safe/manual behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>RECOVERY / REVALIDATION</td>\n<td>Primary source has returned but trust is not yet re-established.</td>\n<td>Observe and compare source; prevent or limit influence until acceptance criteria are met.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>NORMAL RESTORED</td>\n<td>Recovery criteria satisfied.</td>\n<td>Controlled reintegration and return to normal capability.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><div class=\"callout-body\"><figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: The PNT Manager and State Machine\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m07-image4.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure></div></aside>"}, {"id": "failover-is-a-controlled-transition", "title": "Failover Is a Controlled Transition", "html": "<p>Failover should be designed as a transition, not merely a switch. The outgoing and incoming sources may have different reference frames, update rates, biases, uncertainties and transient behaviour.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m07-define-the-trigger\">9.1 Define the trigger</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Hard loss of data or signal.</p></li>\n<li><p>Integrity alarm or explicit source-health failure.</p></li>\n<li><p>Cross-source inconsistency exceeding a defined threshold.</p></li>\n<li><p>Stale corrections or timing data.</p></li>\n<li><p>Uncertainty exceeding the requirement.</p></li>\n<li><p>Operator/system command based on known threat conditions.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m07-define-the-transition\">9.2 Define the transition</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Which source is excluded or down-weighted?</p></li>\n<li><p>What source(s) become authoritative?</p></li>\n<li><p>How is state continuity maintained without an unsafe position/time jump?</p></li>\n<li><p>What uncertainty applies immediately after transition?</p></li>\n<li><p>What alert/mode status is exposed to the application?</p></li>\n<li><p>What application restrictions activate?</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m07-define-the-sustainment-period\">9.3 Define the sustainment period</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>How fast does the fallback solution drift?</p></li>\n<li><p>How long can it meet the required bound?</p></li>\n<li><p>What additional observations can constrain it?</p></li>\n<li><p>What condition forces transition to a more restrictive state?</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-example\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Failover requirement example</p><div class=\"callout-body\">If trusted GNSS is lost, transition to inertial/odometry navigation within 2 seconds, preserve position continuity, declare DEGRADED mode, and maintain horizontal position error below the mission-defined bound for the required outage duration.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "hard-loss-scenario-gnss-disappears", "title": "Hard Loss Scenario: GNSS Disappears", "html": "<p>Consider a vehicle using GNSS + INS + wheel odometry. GNSS is healthy and continuously constrains inertial drift. The vehicle then enters a denied RF area and GNSS measurements disappear.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Stage</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Architecture behaviour</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>1. Detect</td>\n<td>GNSS tracking/measurements disappear; source monitor marks GNSS unavailable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>2. Isolate</td>\n<td>GNSS is removed from fusion because no valid measurements remain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3. Fail over</td>\n<td>INS propagates the state; odometry constrains motion. The PNT manager enters DEGRADED mode.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4. Quantify</td>\n<td>Uncertainty grows according to inertial/odometry performance and elapsed outage time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5. Restrict if necessary</td>\n<td>If uncertainty approaches the application limit, enter RESTRICTED mode or inhibit affected functions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6. Recover</td>\n<td>When GNSS returns, enter revalidation before reintegration; compare against propagated state and source-health checks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7. Restore</td>\n<td>Once acceptance criteria are met, gradually reintegrate GNSS and return to NORMAL.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><div class=\"callout-body\"><figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Hard Loss Scenario: GNSS Disappears\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m07-image5.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure></div></aside>"}, {"id": "deception-scenario-gnss-looks-healthy-but-becomes-suspect", "title": "Deception Scenario: GNSS Looks Healthy but Becomes Suspect", "html": "<p>This scenario is harder because the source has not disappeared. GNSS continues to output a plausible position, but it begins diverging from inertial, odometry and map constraints.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Stage</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Architecture behaviour</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>1. Detect inconsistency</td>\n<td>Cross-source monitor identifies residuals/disagreement beyond expected uncertainty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>2. Do not average blindly</td>\n<td>Fusion must not simply compromise between the suspect GNSS and independent sources.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3. Isolate / quarantine</td>\n<td>GNSS influence is removed or constrained while the anomaly is assessed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4. Maintain service</td>\n<td>INS/odometry/other independent sources provide a degraded solution with explicit uncertainty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5. Alert</td>\n<td>Application receives suspect-source and degraded-integrity state, not just a new coordinate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6. Revalidate</td>\n<td>If GNSS later agrees again, it remains in a recovery state until defined consistency/health criteria are satisfied.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7. Reinstate</td>\n<td>Controlled reintegration prevents a returning bad source from immediately pulling the solution away from truth.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Critical resilience behaviour</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A robust architecture must be able to distrust a source that is still producing data. Availability alone is not evidence of integrity.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "recovery-re-establishing-trust", "title": "Recovery: Re-establishing Trust", "html": "<p>Recovery is often under-engineered. After an outage, receivers may reacquire signals and begin producing solutions quickly. That does not necessarily mean the original cause has disappeared or the state is trustworthy.</p>\n<p>Recovery criteria should be source- and mission-specific. Possible criteria include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Stable source-health indicators for a defined observation period.</p></li>\n<li><p>Agreement with independent sources within expected uncertainty.</p></li>\n<li><p>Fresh authenticated/validated data where supported.</p></li>\n<li><p>Correction data within age/latency limits.</p></li>\n<li><p>Consistent position, velocity, time and clock behaviour.</p></li>\n<li><p>No continuing interference/integrity alarms.</p></li>\n<li><p>Resolution of reference-frame, ambiguity or calibration state as applicable.</p></li>\n<li><p>Application-specific acceptance criteria satisfied before full capability is restored.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Hysteresis prevents mode flapping</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Use different criteria or persistence periods for declaring failure and declaring recovery where appropriate. Otherwise a marginal source can repeatedly enter and leave the solution, creating unstable behaviour.</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Recovery objectives</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Use mission-derived recovery measures. A recovery point objective can express the maximum tolerable PNT error or state loss during disruption, while a recovery time objective expresses how quickly acceptable service must be restored. These are application-specific engineering measures, not universal PNT constants.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "holdover-and-bridging-architectures", "title": "Holdover and Bridging Architectures", "html": "<p>Some PNT functions can be sustained temporarily without a replacement absolute source. INS bridges position/navigation outages; local clocks bridge timing outages. The design problem becomes managing how uncertainty grows and when the bridge is no longer adequate.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Capability</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Primary reference</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Bridge / holdover</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What grows during outage</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Navigation</td>\n<td>GNSS / terrestrial absolute position</td>\n<td>INS + odometry + map/vision constraints</td>\n<td>Position/attitude uncertainty and drift.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Timing</td>\n<td>GNSS / terrestrial/network time</td>\n<td>OCXO / atomic clock / disciplined local clock</td>\n<td>Time offset uncertainty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>High-accuracy position</td>\n<td>RTK/PPP correction service</td>\n<td>Standalone GNSS or inertial integration</td>\n<td>Position uncertainty; loss of fixed/precise mode.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Local positioning</td>\n<td>Infrastructure anchors</td>\n<td>Inertial/odometry</td>\n<td>Relative-position uncertainty.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Real-world timing principle</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A resilient timing service can combine independent distribution paths with local high-stability clocks so a node continues through network-reference loss. The exact holdover duration must be tied to the allowed timing error, not treated as an unlimited backup.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "resilient-timing-architecture-example", "title": "Resilient Timing Architecture Example", "html": "<p>Timing resilience deserves explicit architecture because many systems can operate with no navigation requirement at all. A robust timing design can use a trusted national/primary timescale, independent distribution paths, local disciplined clocks and holdover.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Resilient Timing Architecture Example\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m07-image6.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<p>NPLTime is a useful public example of the principle: NPL describes independent PTP feeds referenced to separate hydrogen masers and a local caesium clock at each service node that can provide extended holdover if the PTP signal is lost. The lesson is architectural: independent feeds plus local holdover and monitoring reduce reliance on a single distribution path.</p>"}, {"id": "avoiding-hidden-single-points-of-failure", "title": "Avoiding Hidden Single Points of Failure", "html": "<p>A resilient design review should trace dependencies below the headline source level. The revised threat module also highlighted supply-chain and third-party dependencies, so the architecture model should include external services, suppliers and upstream references rather than stopping at the receiver boundary.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><div class=\"callout-body\"><figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Avoiding Hidden Single Points of Failure\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m07-image7.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure></div></aside>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Area to inspect</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Typical hidden dependency</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>RF reception</td>\n<td>Multiple GNSS constellations share one antenna, cable or front end.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Power</td>\n<td>Primary and backup receivers use one supply or breaker.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Processing</td>\n<td>All sources are fused by one unmonitored software process.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>Several “independent” services ultimately trace to the same GNSS reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Network</td>\n<td>Two correction/time services traverse the same carrier, router or cloud region.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Reference data</td>\n<td>Visual/terrain alternatives use one corrupted or stale database.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Software / supply chain</td>\n<td>Redundant devices run identical firmware with the same defect.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Physical location</td>\n<td>Co-located sources experience the same interference, fire, flood or damage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operator interface</td>\n<td>All source health is hidden behind one “position valid” flag.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Design review habit</p><div class=\"callout-body\">For every backup, ask: what does it share with the primary? Then ask whether that shared element is itself monitored, redundant or acceptable as a residual risk.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "sensor-fusion-for-resilience", "title": "Sensor Fusion for Resilience", "html": "<p>Fusion improves continuity and performance when sources complement one another, but resilience requires the estimator to understand source uncertainty and faults. Simply adding measurements can make a solution worse if a faulty source dominates the estimate.</p>\n<p>A resilient fusion design should consider:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Per-source uncertainty models that reflect actual operating conditions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Innovation/residual monitoring and plausibility checks.</p></li>\n<li><p>Fault exclusion or robust weighting when measurements become inconsistent.</p></li>\n<li><p>Separate handling of correlated measurements and shared dependencies.</p></li>\n<li><p>Mode-aware behaviour when sources are lost or restored.</p></li>\n<li><p>Propagation of solution uncertainty to the consuming application.</p></li>\n<li><p>Test cases where the “wrong” source is more confident than the correct source.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">A useful adversarial test</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Inject a plausible but biased source while an independent source remains correct. Does the estimator identify the inconsistency, or does the high-confidence bad measurement pull the fused solution away from truth?</div></aside>"}, {"id": "designing-the-application-interface", "title": "Designing the Application Interface", "html": "<p>A resilient PNT subsystem can still fail operationally if the application receives only latitude, longitude and time. The consumer needs enough context to decide whether the information is suitable for the current function.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>PNT value/state (position, velocity, time, attitude as applicable).</p></li>\n<li><p>Estimated uncertainty or protection bound.</p></li>\n<li><p>Integrity/trust state.</p></li>\n<li><p>Current operating mode: normal, degraded, restricted, recovery.</p></li>\n<li><p>Source health or source set where operationally useful.</p></li>\n<li><p>Age/freshness of solution and corrections.</p></li>\n<li><p>Alerts and reason codes for major transitions.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Application-level resilience</p><div class=\"callout-body\">The application should define what it does when PNT quality falls. A navigation subsystem cannot guarantee safety if the consuming autonomy stack ignores its degraded-state flag.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "engineering-evidence-and-testing", "title": "Engineering Evidence and Testing", "html": "<p>Resilience claims should be demonstrated under controlled normal, degraded and recovery conditions. Module 9 will cover assurance in depth, but architecture decisions should already be testable.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Evidence area</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example questions</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Dependency analysis</td>\n<td>Have shared antennas, power, networks, timing, software, suppliers and upstream services been mapped?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Supply-chain assurance</td>\n<td>Are component provenance, update paths, third-party roles, known limitations and fallback arrangements understood?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Failure injection</td>\n<td>Has each source been lost, delayed, frozen, biased or made inconsistent in representative tests?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Detection</td>\n<td>Is time-to-detect measured, including false alarms and missed detections?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Failover</td>\n<td>Are transition time, discontinuity and degraded performance measured?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Holdover</td>\n<td>Does error growth remain within the requirement for the stated outage duration?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Can a returning source be prevented from premature reintegration?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Application response</td>\n<td>Does the consuming system actually reduce/inhibit functions when integrity is insufficient?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operational environment</td>\n<td>Have representative dynamics, RF conditions, geography, weather and infrastructure failures been included?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "architecture-review-checklist", "title": "Architecture Review Checklist", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>What PNT functions and performance must survive?</p></li>\n<li><p>What is the maximum credible outage/deception duration?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which sources are primary, complementary and fallback?</p></li>\n<li><p>How independent are they physically, logically and operationally?</p></li>\n<li><p>How is each source monitored before fusion?</p></li>\n<li><p>How are faults detected, excluded and isolated?</p></li>\n<li><p>How is uncertainty propagated through degraded modes?</p></li>\n<li><p>What triggers failover, restriction and safe-state transitions?</p></li>\n<li><p>How long can holdover/bridging meet the requirement?</p></li>\n<li><p>What does the application receive about integrity and mode?</p></li>\n<li><p>What are the explicit recovery and revalidation criteria?</p></li>\n<li><p>What shared dependencies or single points of failure remain?</p></li>\n<li><p>What supplier, correction-service, network, cloud or upstream timing dependencies could defeat the fallback?</p></li>\n<li><p>What maximum tolerable PNT error/state loss and recovery time does the mission require?</p></li>\n<li><p>What test evidence demonstrates the complete failure → failover → recovery sequence?</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "speaking-like-a-resilient-pnt-engineer", "title": "Speaking Like a Resilient-PNT Engineer", "html": "<p>When presented with a “resilient PNT” architecture, useful questions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Which operational requirement is the resilience claim tied to?</p></li>\n<li><p>What failure/threat scenarios were used to design the architecture?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which sources are genuinely independent, and what do they share?</p></li>\n<li><p>Can the system detect a plausible but wrong source, or only total loss?</p></li>\n<li><p>What are the fault-detection and isolation mechanisms?</p></li>\n<li><p>What triggers failover and how long does transition take?</p></li>\n<li><p>What capability remains after failover, for how long and with what uncertainty?</p></li>\n<li><p>What happens if the fallback source also degrades?</p></li>\n<li><p>How does the consuming application know it is in degraded/restricted mode?</p></li>\n<li><p>What prevents a returning source being trusted immediately?</p></li>\n<li><p>How are recovery and revalidation demonstrated?</p></li>\n<li><p>What evidence shows the architecture behaves correctly under representative simultaneous/common-mode failures?</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>Resilient PNT is an engineered end-to-end behaviour, not a second receiver or a marketing label.</p></li>\n<li><p>Requirements must define what PNT capability survives, at what performance, for how long and with what detection/recovery behaviour.</p></li>\n<li><p>Redundancy is strengthened by diversity, dissimilarity and genuine independence from common failure paths.</p></li>\n<li><p>Robust architectures monitor each source before fusion and use FDE/FDI to prevent suspect information contaminating the solution.</p></li>\n<li><p>A supervisory PNT manager should control source health, mode transitions, failover, degraded operation and recovery.</p></li>\n<li><p>Failover must define triggers, transition behaviour, resulting uncertainty and application restrictions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Hard source loss and plausible-but-wrong information require different detection behaviour.</p></li>\n<li><p>Holdover and dead reckoning bridge outages, but their uncertainty grows and must be tied to explicit duration/performance requirements.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recovery requires revalidation and controlled reintegration rather than immediate trust of a returning source.</p></li>\n<li><p>The application must receive integrity, uncertainty and mode information so it can degrade safely.</p></li>\n<li><p>Resilience should be decomposed into resistance, detection, response and recovery functions against defined threats.</p></li>\n<li><p>Supply-chain and upstream-service dependencies must be included when judging source independence.</p></li>\n<li><p>Resilience claims should be demonstrated through failure injection, failover, sustainment, common-mode and recovery testing.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Working definition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Resilient PNT</td>\n<td>Ability engineered into a system to maintain acceptable trustworthy PNT through disruption and recover under defined criteria.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Redundancy</td>\n<td>Provision of more than one component/source capable of supporting a required function.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Diversity</td>\n<td>Use of alternatives differing in technology, source, implementation or supplier.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Dissimilarity</td>\n<td>Use of sufficiently different physical principles or failure mechanisms to reduce common-mode exposure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Independence</td>\n<td>Degree to which one source can remain valid when another fails because shared dependencies are limited/controlled.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>FDE</td>\n<td>Fault Detection and Exclusion: identifying inconsistent information and excluding it from the solution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>FDI</td>\n<td>Fault Detection and Isolation: detecting a fault and identifying/isolation of the affected source/component.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Failover</td>\n<td>Controlled transition from a failed/suspect capability to an alternate capability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Holdover</td>\n<td>Temporary continuation of timing using a local clock after loss of an external reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Bridging</td>\n<td>Maintaining a capability across a temporary loss using a propagating/local source such as INS or a clock.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Graceful degradation</td>\n<td>Controlled reduction in capability as available PNT performance or integrity decreases.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Revalidation</td>\n<td>Checks performed before a recovered source is trusted and reintegrated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hysteresis</td>\n<td>Use of different/persistent transition criteria to prevent rapid oscillation between system states.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT manager</td>\n<td>Supervisory function controlling source health, selection, modes, failover, alerts and recovery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Integrity state</td>\n<td>Indication of whether the current PNT solution is sufficiently trustworthy for its intended use.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning", "title": "Further Learning", "html": "<p><strong>UK Government: Positioning, Navigation and Timing Overview</strong><br/>\nNational PNT Office overview and the UK Framework for Greater PNT Resilience, including resilient timing, eLoran, holdover and crisis planning.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/positioning-navigation-and-timing-overview\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/guidance/positioning-navigation-and-timing-overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Space Strategy</strong><br/>\nCurrent UK direction toward layered PNT systems that are independent and not vulnerable to the same threats.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-space-strategy/uk-space-strategy\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-space-strategy/uk-space-strategy</a></p>\n<p><strong>NIST IR 8323 Rev. 2: Foundational PNT Profile</strong><br/>\n2026 draft profile for governing PNT risk, identifying appropriate sources, protecting equipment, detecting manipulation, responding and recovering.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8323/r2/ipd\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8323/r2/ipd</a></p>\n<p><strong>NIST: Responsible Use of PNT Services</strong><br/>\nPNT resilience and cybersecurity resources for risk-informed use of PNT services.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/pnt\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.nist.gov/pnt</a></p>\n<p><strong>NPLTime: Time and Frequency FAQs</strong><br/>\nPublic example of resilient timing architecture using independent PTP feeds and local caesium holdover.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.npl.co.uk/products-services/time-frequency/npltime/frequently-asked-questions\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.npl.co.uk/products-services/time-frequency/npltime/frequently-asked-questions</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Government: Framework for Greater PNT Resilience announcement</strong><br/>\nUK commitments including National Timing Centre, eLoran, resilient receiver technology and holdover clocks.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/news/critical-services-to-be-better-protected-from-satellite-data-disruptions-through-new-position-navigation-and-timing-framework\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/news/critical-services-to-be-better-protected-from-satellite-data-disruptions-through-new-position-navigation-and-timing-framework</a></p>\n<p><strong>CISA/DOT: PNT Backup and Complementary Capabilities</strong><br/>\nDiscussion of complementary PNT capabilities and why shared antenna/chipset approaches may offer only partial resilience.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/publications/report-on-pnt-backup-complementary-capabilities-to-gps_508_0.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/publications/report-on-pnt-backup-complementary-capabilities-to-gps_508_0.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>RethinkPNT: PNT System Resilience</strong><br/>\nDetailed treatment of resilience as resistance, detection, response and recovery, with emphasis on threat-relative system behaviour.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://rethinkpnt.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Website_copy_PNT-System-Resilience.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rethinkpnt.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Website_copy_PNT-System-Resilience.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>DHS / CISA: Resilient PNT Conformance Framework v2.0</strong><br/>\nOutcome-based framework showing that resilient PNT depends on system architecture and expected behaviours, not individual components alone.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2022-05/22_0531_st_resilient_pnt_conformance_framework_v2.0.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2022-05/22_0531_st_resilient_pnt_conformance_framework_v2.0.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>Royal Institute of Navigation: PNT Resilience Checklist</strong><br/>\nConcise prompts for identifying dependencies, independent alternatives, outage behaviour and resilience testing.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://rin.org.uk/mpage/Resilient_PNT_Checklist\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rin.org.uk/mpage/Resilient_PNT_Checklist</a></p>\n<p><strong>Royal Institute of Navigation: SALHUB PNT Training</strong><br/>\nFurther professional learning relevant to PNT systems engineering, sensor integration, timing and resilience.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://rin.org.uk/event/SALHUBOnlinePNT\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rin.org.uk/event/SALHUBOnlinePNT</a></p>"}, {"id": "next-module", "title": "Next Module", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MODULE 08 — PNT ARCHITECTURE &amp; SYSTEMS ENGINEERING</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>Module 8 moves from the resilient reference pattern into broader systems engineering. Learners will translate operational needs into PNT requirements, build dependency and interface models, make architecture trade-offs, define reference frames and timing interfaces, allocate requirements across subsystems, manage assumptions and constraints, and evaluate supplier architectures against mission outcomes.</p>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "Which best describes resilient PNT?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Installing two GPS receivers."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Engineering the system to maintain acceptable trustworthy PNT through disruption, manage degraded modes and recover under defined criteria."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Always selecting the most accurate source."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Preventing every possible PNT failure."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Resilience is an end-to-end behaviour tied to acceptable capability, trust, mode transitions and recovery."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Which characteristics strengthen source resilience?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Redundancy."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Diversity."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Dissimilarity."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Independence from common failure paths."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Different product branding only."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "Source count helps only when alternatives reduce common failure exposure and can genuinely sustain the function."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "A GNSS source is still outputting plausible positions but disagrees materially with INS and odometry. What is the most resilient response?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Average all sources regardless."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Treat availability as proof of trust."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Detect the inconsistency, isolate/quarantine the suspect source and continue using validated alternatives under an appropriate mode."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Immediately declare the INS correct forever."}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "A resilient architecture must be able to distrust a source that remains available and prevent it contaminating the solution."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "What should a failover design define?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Trigger criteria."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Which source becomes authoritative."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Transition behaviour and discontinuity."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Degraded uncertainty/performance."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Application alerts/mode changes."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Failover includes detection, transition, resulting capability and communication to the consumer."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "Why should a returning primary source normally pass through revalidation?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "To make recovery slower."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Because reacquisition/availability alone does not prove the original fault has cleared or the source is trustworthy."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Because backup sources must never be stopped."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Because GNSS cannot recover after an outage."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Recovery requires trust to be re-established, not merely data to reappear."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "Which are possible hidden common-mode dependencies?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Shared antenna."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Shared power."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Same correction network."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Same upstream time reference."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Same software defect."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "All can cause nominally redundant components to fail or become wrong together."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "What is the role of FDE/FDI?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Increase satellite transmit power."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Detect inconsistent/faulty information and prevent or limit it from corrupting the solution."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Replace all sensor fusion."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Guarantee the cause of every anomaly is known immediately."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "FDE/FDI protects the state estimate by detecting and excluding/isolation suspect information; root-cause attribution can follow later."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "What does holdover mean in a timing architecture?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The clock is permanently independent of all references."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The local clock maintains time after loss of an external reference, with uncertainty growing over time."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The system stops time until the reference returns."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The network sends more packets."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Holdover bridges reference loss but its error must be bounded against a time-duration requirement."}, {"n": 9, "prompt": "What should an application ideally receive from a resilient PNT service?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "PNT values."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Uncertainty/integrity information."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Operating mode or degraded-state indication."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Freshness/age where relevant."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Alerts for important transitions."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Applications need quality and state information so they can adapt behaviour safely."}, {"n": 10, "prompt": "Which tests support a robust resilience claim?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Hard loss of a primary source."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Plausible biased/suspect source."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Fallback drift/holdover over required duration."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Recovery and premature-reintegration prevention."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Common-mode/shared-dependency failures."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Resilience must be demonstrated across denial, deception-like inconsistency, sustainment, common-mode failure and recovery."}]});
